WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.330
- The technology you created

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.705
has linked this poor creature

00:00:04.705 --> 00:00:06.790
(gentle pensive music)

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.080
to a certain obscenity,

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.830
what do you have to say to it?

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:11.650
- I'm very, very sorry.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:12.800
I never meant it

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.050
but it's all my ducking fault.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.430
- Those of us who
sometimes get the word duck

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.250
instead of a certain
similar sounding curse word

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.710
on our iPhones,

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.460
can blame this guy.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.577
- My name is Ken Kocienda,

00:00:24.577 --> 00:00:27.450
and I invented autocorrect for the iPhone.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.147
- Oh yes, I really came all
the way out to this farm

00:00:30.147 --> 00:00:32.070
(music)

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.530
and teamed up with these adorable guys

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.310
to bash on autocorrect.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.020
Of course, Dr. Ellen Crain,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.450
the owner of the Safe
Haven Farm Sanctuary,

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:41.590
has been here too

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:45.300
to ensure that these rescued
ducks are happy, healthy,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.380
and definitely not hungry.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.213
Okay, fine.

00:00:48.213 --> 00:00:51.070
I really came out here
to answer two questions:

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.700
why in certain situations

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.150
does Apple software autocorrect something

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.400
when you clearly meant
to type something else,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.310
and number two,

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.810
has Apple software gotten worse with time?

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.650
That's why I track down Ken.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.890
He spent 15 years working in Apple

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.840
and even wrote a book about his experience

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.640
of working on the iPhone.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.910
So I ran him and some other experts

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.410
through some recent autocorrect fails,

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:17.953
to find out what the duck is going on.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.690
Fail number one, random word changes.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:24.030
- I was gonna try to write software

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.700
that looked at what people did

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.410
and changed the letters
out from under them.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.920
And so when I did that,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.443
I wanted to be as sure as I could be

00:01:32.443 --> 00:01:34.670
that I was giving them what they wanted,

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:38.000
not some ridiculous result
that they didn't want.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.550
- But that's not how it
always works anymore.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.280
Let's take one of my
recent examples to explain.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.050
I use a tool at work called NewsGrid,

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:49.203
but autocorrect always
changes it to newsgirl.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.580
As you type, the autocorrect algorithms

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.330
try to figure out what you mean

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.220
by looking at various things,

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.290
including where your fingers hit the keys

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.280
and other words in the sentence,

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.160
all the while it's comparing your words

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.490
to the words in its dictionaries.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.100
- System that I built

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.963
actually has two dictionaries in it.

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:13.630
The product shipped with what
I called a static dictionary,

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.390
you know, a standard dictionary.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.970
It would include all common words

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.660
and it would gonna include
things like names for products

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.130
and sports teams.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.200
- [Joanna] Then there
was a second dictionary.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.060
- I called it a dynamic dictionary,

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.740
and it was just this
running list of your words

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.520
and your custom words.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.520
The purpose of it was to
try to help you type words

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.220
that were in your own personal vocabulary,

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.030
words that were different

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.680
than words from standard English.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.010
- [Joanna] So in the case of NewsGrid,

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.420
the system is going to
the static dictionary

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.810
and replacing it with a
word it knows, newsgirl,

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.660
but it really needs to add NewsGrid

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.700
to the dynamic dictionary.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.320
This explains other issues

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.190
like when autocorrect changes a word

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.570
to the name of someone
in your address book,

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.700
or the name of an app on your phone.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.000
All of that info is part
of your dynamic dictionary.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.380
- And so then from that point forward,

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.780
the static dictionary and
the dynamic dictionary

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.850
would be kind of in a
little bit of a battle

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:16.810
with each other,

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.490
and it was the ultimate job
of the autocorrection software

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.060
to break ties.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.430
- [Joanna] After you type
a word more than two times,

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.510
the system is designed to learn that word

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.730
and stopped correcting it any further.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:29.650
With NewsGrid,

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:31.470
that hasn't exactly happened.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.510
Apple said different capitalization

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.790
could impact that.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.700
But here's how you can fix
an error like that for good,

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.270
go to Settings, General,

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:42.500
Keyboard, Text Replacements

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.850
and add the word you want

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.560
to the Phrase and Shortcuts fields.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.750
Now, when you type this word,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.720
autocorrect should respect your typing.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.370
Fail number two, no bad words.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.810
This is a duck in spilled
water on my keyboard.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.040
I certainly did not
have this image in mind

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.370
when I sent it to a
friend a few months ago.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.361
Of course I wanted to say...

00:04:05.361 --> 00:04:08.000
(keypad clicking)

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.970
Here's why that happens.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.680
- I promise you,

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.150
on the original iPhone,

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.980
that if you typed a
certain four letter word

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:16.980
that begins with F,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.900
if you typed it exactly,

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.970
the keyboard would leave it alone,

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.930
so you could have your curses.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.860
But if you missed one of those letters,

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:30.370
it wasn't going to
correct to the curse word.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.250
- [Joanna] Apple did that

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.650
because it didn't want you
to ever mistakenly type

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:35.180
a curse word or slur,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.310
or another inappropriate word.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.440
- And so I had to do a study

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.010
to go out and find all the terrible things

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.340
that people would say

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:48.760
and actually put them
in the static dictionary

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.400
but with a special mark on them

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.740
to say never help
anybody type these words.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.570
If they type them exactly, okay,

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.630
but if they make a mistake,

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:03.520
don't use autocorrect to nudge somebody

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.520
towards saying something awful.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.750
- So what can you do about this?

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:11.609
Well, you can type your
bad words correctly.

00:05:11.609 --> 00:05:14.283
Somebody get this bug.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.880
You can also add those
words to that dictionary

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.610
or your contacts list.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.160
Bye, bye, ducks.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.230
Fail number three, grammatical errors.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:27.080
I ran Ken through a couple
of recent ones I've seen.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:30.870
Sometimes I'll type W-E-L-L, well

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:35.440
but it becomes we'll, W-E-apostrophe L-L.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.620
- Well and we'll is
particularly challenging

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:42.030
because both of those
are really common words.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.160
My decision back then

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.890
was you needed to type the apostrophe

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:46.500
in order to get we'll.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.360
- [Joanna] Sometimes the
system changes in to on,

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:52.230
or they're just strange
suggestions like and's.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.120
Over time, the system can build

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.780
became more sophisticated and aggressive,

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:00.960
correcting words based on the
previous words in a sentence.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.180
Complex machine learning

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:04.660
based on what people have
been typing on the internet

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.230
gave the system lots of new data

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.720
on what people might be trying to type.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:09.720
In iOS 15,

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.710
it's now learning from what
users type on their iPhones,

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:15.920
though it's all designed in
a privacy respecting way.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.840
- It's an interesting challenge
because a lot of times,

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:22.290
words, new words come into the language.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.030
You know, with the idea
that you're going to train

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.920
a, you know, essentially a web crawler

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.810
to go out and look at the
words that people are typing,

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.390
typos are gonna get into the system,

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:35.240
and so how do you get them out?

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:38.140
Well, it's probably a process

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:41.240
that humans are gonna
have to go after the fact

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:42.480
and clean those things up.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:43.920
- So what can you do?

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.210
One option, not use
Apple's keyboard at all.

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:49.900
You can download a third party
option from the App Store,

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:51.440
like Google's Gboard.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.060
One called Typewise is pretty wacky,

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.740
but I love that it has an
autocorrect undo button

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.620
right on the board.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:03.160
Okay, so has autocorrect
actually gotten worse?

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.550
Well, it certainly seems
like machine learning

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:09.240
has added some more complication
to the whole system.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.490
- And so the system has improved a lot

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.550
because it's been asked to do more

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:17.600
but therein lies the rub of like features

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.120
and software systems on
computers, on gadgets.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:22.730
The more you ask it to do,

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:24.910
the more potential there is for bugs

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:26.770
and unexpected behaviors.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:28.470
- There may be something to that,

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:30.880
try turning off autocorrect yourself.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.710
I bet the farm that you
will quickly realize

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:36.400
how much good work it's doing for you,

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.240
despite the occasional duck up.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.850
All right, guys, for the first time,

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.830
I am going to send a bunch
of messages to people

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:47.923
where I actually mean to
say duck or ducking today.

